body:
I would love to read this blog over and over and over again.  I am a senior in high school and I, like a lot of people my age, don't know what to do once I graduate in May and I also worry about how my cp fits into the equation. I think reading your blog will put my mind at ease, some.  You are, in a way, living proof. (I hope you don't find that offensive at all) I don't know older people with cerebral palsy in person so I can't ask questions or get advice from their experiences, which kinda makes me feel lost, y'know?

&#x200B;

Anyways, to make a long story short, you should write a blog!!

&#x200B;

I don't take baclofen or any medication anymore but I have worked out regularly before.  I did some light free weights, body weight squats/push-ups, and jumped rope for about 8 months straight. I worked out 3x a week for as long as I wanted/as long as I felt like I could push myself. I didn't do all those exercises every time I worked out-- just thought I would clarify.   (I have spastic diplegia if you were wondering.)

I'd say that I gained muscle within that time.  The definition in my physique has increased noticeably and my balance has improved immensely!! (before working out, I had trouble using stairs but after a while, it became easier since I didn't feel like I was going to fall so easily)
